"""Release 3.0.1: drops (NOT applied in 3.0; owner review required first)

Revision ID: r30_04_drops
Revises: r30_03_data
Create Date: 2026-09-23 (drafted, not wired into the alembic chain)

This file is intentionally suffixed .py.draft, not .py, so alembic does not
discover it and it does NOT create a second head. Rename to r30_04_drops.py
only after the owner has confirmed each item below (07_database.md
recommendation 10) and stream F re-verifies violator counts on prod
read-only right before applying, the same way r30_02/r30_03 were verified
2026-09-23.

Planned for 3.0.1, once the streams that touch these areas have landed and
had a full release cycle to prove nothing needs them:

1. Drop dead tables (0 rows, no code outside `legacy/` per 07 §1.1/§2.1):
   access_requests, squads, nodes.
2. Drop dead columns (all-NULL/constant on every prod row, 07 §1.2 D-notes):
   subscriptions.remna_subscription_id, subscriptions.last_expiry_notice_at
   (unless stream C implements the expiry-notice rate limit it was meant
   for -- check before dropping), telegram_users.last_activity_at (unless
   stream D's auth middleware starts writing it), telegram_users.is_admin,
   remna_users.email, remna_users.last_synced_at.
3. Drop ~20 unused/low-cardinality indexes (07 §3), including the duplicate
   ix_payments_external_id (payments_external_id_key already covers it).
4. NOT included here (data risk, owner sign-off needed separately, not a
   schema drop): NULLing remna_users.raw_data for the 5 rows holding live
   VPN credentials (07 §2.5), and resolving subscriptions 22/87 (active but
   valid_until in the past, 07 Q1) -- that one needs a real read from
   Remnawave, not a blind SQL UPDATE, so it belongs with stream B/C's
   reconciler fix (07 recommendation 6), not a stream F migration.

Example upgrade() body (untested against prod, write NOT VALID/VALIDATE-free
since DROP does not need it -- but drop each object one statement at a time,
recheck idempotency with IF EXISTS, and run scripts/prod_db_dump_and_restore_test.sh
against a fresh dump before touching prod):

    op.execute("DROP TABLE IF EXISTS access_requests")
    op.execute("DROP TABLE IF EXISTS squads")
    op.execute("DROP TABLE IF EXISTS nodes")
    op.execute("ALTER TABLE subscriptions DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS remna_subscription_id")
    op.execute("ALTER TABLE subscriptions DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS last_expiry_notice_at")
    op.execute("ALTER TABLE telegram_users DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS last_activity_at")
    op.execute("ALTER TABLE telegram_users DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS is_admin")
    op.execute("ALTER TABLE remna_users DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS email")
    op.execute("ALTER TABLE remna_users DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS last_synced_at")
    op.execute("DROP INDEX IF EXISTS ix_payments_external_id")
    # ... plus the rest of the ~20 unused indexes listed in 07_database.md §3

Matching model.py edits (drop the columns/relationships/indexes) must land
in the SAME commit as the real migration, per ARCHITECTURE_3.0.md's rule
"every migration: model updated in the same commit".
"""
